#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# distance_matrix

#% text
# `distance_matrix(points)` gives the matrix of Euclidean distances between the points.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `distance_matrix(a, b)` gives the distances between the points of `a` and the points of `b`.

#% item
# The points are vectors or numbers.

#% item
# The points may be given as an association of equal-length columns (or a dataset of one), whose rows are the points.

#% item
# `feature_types` names the type of each feature (column) by position, in an association or a list: `"Nominal"` (a mismatch counts 1) or `"Numerical"` (a difference). With only nominal features the distance is the number of mismatches; with a mixture it is the square root of the sum of the squared differences and the mismatches. A column not named is nominal when some entry of it is not a number.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
distance_matrix([[0, 0], [3, 4], [1, 1]])
#% output
#= [[0, 5, 2^(1/2)], [5, 0, 13^(1/2)], [2^(1/2), 13^(1/2), 0]]

#% cell
distance_matrix([1, 4, 9])
#% output
#= [[0, 3, 8], [3, 0, 5], [8, 5, 0]]

#% cell
distance_matrix([[0, 0], [1, 0]], [[0, 1], [1, 1], [2, 2]])
#% output
#= [[1, 2^(1/2), 2*2^(1/2)], [2^(1/2), 1, 5^(1/2)]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% cell
distance_matrix(assoc("age" -> [32, 41, 30], "gender" -> ["f", "f", "m"]))
#% output
#= [[0, 9, 5^(1/2)], [9, 0, 122^(1/2)], [5^(1/2), 122^(1/2), 0]]

#% cell
distance_matrix([[1, "A"], [2, "A"], [2, "B"], [1, "B"]], feature_types: assoc(1 -> "Nominal"))
#% output
#= [[0, 1, 2, 1], [1, 0, 1, 2], [2, 1, 0, 1], [1, 2, 1, 0]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `euclidean_distance`, `manhattan_distance`, `nearest`
